Implementing a multi-tree list in an Angular application involves several steps. A tree list is essentially a hierarchical data structure that allows for easy navigation and filtering of items at different levels.

Here’s a basic implementation using Angular Material's `MatTreeGrid` component, which provides a multi-level tree structure:

### Step 1: Install Angular Material

First, make sure you have Angular Material installed. If not, add it to your project via npm or yarn:

```bash
npm install @angular/material @angular/cdk --save
```

Or if you're using Yarn:

```bash
yarn add @angular/material @angular/cdk
```

### Step 2: Import the Components

In your Angular module (e.g., `app.module.ts`), import the necessary components from Angular Material:

```typescript
import { MatTreeGridModule } from '@angular/material/tree-grid';
```

And then declare them in the imports array:

```typescript
imports: [
    // other modules...
    MatTreeGridModule,
],
```

### Step 3: Create a Component for the Tree List

Create a new Angular component (e.g., `tree-list.component.ts`) to handle the tree list logic:

```typescript
// tree-list.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-tree-list',
  templateUrl: './tree-list.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./tree-list.component.css']
})
export class TreeListComponent {
  nodes = [
    { id: 0, label: 'Root Node', children: [
      { id: 1, label: 'Child Node 1' },
      { id: 2, label: 'Child Node 2' }
    ]},
    { id: 3, label: 'Another Root Node', children: [
      { id: 4, label: 'Grandchild Node 1' },
      { id: 5, label: 'Grandchild Node 2' }
    ]}
  ];

  onNodeClick(node) {
    console.log('Clicked node:', node);
  }
}
```

### Step 4: Define the Template

Next, create the template file (`tree-list.component.html`) to display the tree list:

```html
<!-- tree-list.component.html -->
<mat-tree>
  <ng-template matTreeNodeContent let-node>
    <div [style.background]="node.label">
      {{ node.label }}
      <button (click)="onNodeClick(node)">Click Me</button>
    </div>
  </ng-template>

  <!-- Add more templates here to define child nodes -->
</mat-tree>
```

### Step 5: Initialize the Tree List

Finally, initialize the tree list in your main app component (e.g., `app.component.ts`):

```typescript
// app.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { TreeListComponent } from './tree-list/tree-list.component';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./app.component.css'],
  standalone: true,
  imports: [
    // other modules...
    TreeListComponent,
  ],
})
export class AppComponent {
  title = 'my-app';
}
```

### Step 6: Run Your Application

Make sure your application runs properly:

```bash
ng serve
```

Now you should see a simple multi-tree list where clicking any node will trigger the `onNodeClick` method in the component.

